#3. Pattern Matching for instanceof (Java 16)
=============================================
Before

Object obj = "Java";

if (obj instanceof String) {
    String str = (String) obj;
    System.out.println(str.length());
}

After

Object obj = "Java";

if (obj instanceof String str) {
    System.out.println(str.length());
}

Benefit: Combines type checking and casting, reducing boilerplate.

Interview: Pattern matching for instanceof declares a typed variable when the type check succeeds.

4. Switch Expressions (Java 14)
================================
Before

String result;

switch (day) {
    case 1:
        result = "Monday";
        break;
    case 2:
        result = "Tuesday";
        break;
    default:
        result = "Invalid";
}

After

String result = switch (day) {
    case 1 -> "Monday";
    case 2 -> "Tuesday";
    default -> "Invalid";
};

Benefit: Cleaner syntax, fewer break statements, and switch can return a value.

Interview: Switch expressions make conditional value assignment concise and reduce accidental fall-through.

5. Text Blocks (Java 15)
=========================
Before

String json = "{\n" +
              "  \"name\": \"Ritika\",\n" +
              "  \"age\": 22\n" +
              "}";

After

String json = """
        {
          "name": "Ritika",
          "age": 22
        }
        """;

Benefit: Easier multi-line strings for SQL, JSON, HTML, and messages.

Interview: Text blocks simplify multi-line string literals and reduce escaping.

6. Helpful NullPointerExceptions (Java 14)
==========================================
Before — less descriptive message

String name = null;
System.out.println(name.length());

Possible message:

java.lang.NullPointerException

After — Java 17 JVM can give more detail

Cannot invoke "String.length()" because "name" is null

Benefit: Faster debugging by identifying the null expression.

Interview: Helpful NPEs improve error diagnostics; they don't prevent null errors.

7. RandomGenerator API (Java 17)
================================
Before

Random random = new Random();
int number = random.nextInt(6) + 1;

After

RandomGenerator random =
        RandomGenerator.getDefault();

int number = random.nextInt(1, 7);

Benefit: A common interface supports different random-number algorithms.

Interview: Java 17 enhanced random-number generation with a flexible API for selecting and using algorithms.

8. HexFormat (Java 17)
=======================
Before

byte[] bytes = {10, 15, 16};

StringBuilder result = new StringBuilder();

for (byte b : bytes) {
    result.append(String.format("%02x", b));
}

System.out.println(result); // 0a0f10

After

HexFormat hex = HexFormat.of();

String result = hex.formatHex(
        new byte[]{10, 15, 16}
);

System.out.println(result); // 0a0f10

Benefit: Simple hexadecimal encoding and decoding.

Interview: HexFormat, introduced in Java 17, simplifies converting byte arrays to and from hexadecimal strings.

9. Stream.toList() (Java 16)
============================
Before

List<String> names = List.of("Ritika", "Amit");

List<String> result = names.stream()
        .filter(name -> name.length() > 4)
        .collect(Collectors.toList());

After

List<String> names = List.of("Ritika", "Amit");

List<String> result = names.stream()
        .filter(name -> name.length() > 4)
        .toList();

Benefit: Shorter stream collection syntax.

Interview: Stream.toList() returns an unmodifiable list. Use a collector such as Collectors.toCollection(ArrayList::new) when you need a mutable list.

10. Strong Encapsulation of JDK Internals (Java 17)
===================================================
Before — legacy applications might rely on internal APIs

// Example of internal API usage; avoid this.
import sun.misc.Unsafe;

After — prefer supported public APIs

import java.util.ArrayList;

var names = new ArrayList<String>();
names.add("Ritika");

Benefit: Better maintainability, security, and compatibility by avoiding internal implementation details.

Interview: Java 17 strongly encapsulates JDK internals by default, though some compatibility exceptions exist.

11. Always-Strict Floating-Point Semantics (Java 17)
====================================================
Before — older Java could permit extended-exponent-value behavior in certain contexts

double result = 10.0 / 3.0;
System.out.println(result);

After — same ordinary code

double result = 10.0 / 3.0;
System.out.println(result);

Benefit: Floating-point expressions follow consistent strict semantics across platforms. No syntax change is needed.

Interview: Java 17 restored always-strict floating-point semantics.